{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Were burst open all the fountains of the great deep and the windows of the heavens were opened.</b> It would have been fitting for it to say, 'On this day the windows of the heavens were opened and all the fountains of the great deep were burst open,' for it should have given precedence to the upper waters over the lower waters. And it is possible to say that since the matter was curse and calamity, Scripture did not wish to give them precedence. And we find that Scripture gave them precedence in a context of blessing, as it said (Genesis 49): ״ברכות שמים מעל ברכות תהום רובצת תחת״. And so Moses, peace be upon him, mentioned (Deuteronomy 33): ״ממגד שמים מטל ומתהום רובצת תחת״. And from this very matter it was that Scripture did not mention ״ויביא אלהים מבול על הארץ״ — 'and Elohim brought a flood upon the earth' — forty days and forty nights, for it did not wish to mention HaShem in a context of calamity. And the matter is well known, that He is the one who brings, for He had already said to him ״ואני הנני מביא״ — 'and I, behold I, am bringing.' But the Torah, whose ways are all ways of pleasantness and all her paths are peace — such is its practice that it ascribes the matter to HaShem in matters of calamity and does not mention Him. And likewise what it said ״וימח את כל היקום״ — 'and He blotted out all that existed' — and did not say 'and Elohim blotted out all that existed.' But in deliverance and mercy it mentioned HaShem, as it is written ״ויסגר ה' בעדו״, and it is written ״ויעבר אלהים רוח על הארץ״. And so we find that when He punished Adam and Eve, Scripture said anonymously (Genesis 3): ״אל האשה אמר״, ״ולאדם אמר״ — 'to the woman He said,' 'and to Adam He said' — and did not mention HaShem, blessed be He, in their curse. But with the measure of compassion, when He had compassion upon them and clothed them, it mentioned in connection with them the Name, blessed be He, as it is written (ibid.): ״ויעש ה' אלהים לאדם ולאשתו כתנות עור וילבישם״." "<b>And if</b> you look into the Scriptures, you will find that He does not bring calamity except with difficulty and great compulsion. For at first it said ״ויהי הגשם על הארץ״ — 'and the rain was upon the earth' — and afterward it said ״ויהי המבול״ — 'and the flood was.' And this is to indicate that at first it came down gently, so that if they would repent He would annul His decree and turn the waters to good, and they would be rains of blessing; and if not, it would be a flood. And for this reason all the other living creatures perished in the flood first and human beings last, as it is said ״וכל האדם״ — 'and all mankind' — for just as man was last in the creation of the world, so too was he last in its destruction."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:07.437766957Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}